==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|collacioun}}, {{m|enm|collation}}, from {{der|en|fro|collation}}, from {{der|en|la|collatiō}}, from the participle stem of {{m|la|cōnferō|t=to bring together}}. Not related to {{noncog|en|collateral}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈleɪʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-collation.wav|Audio (RP)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=3}}
* {{homophones|en|colation}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Bringing together.
## The act of bringing things together and [[compare|comparing]] them; [[comparison]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* '''November 8, 1717''', The Bishop of Rochester, ''letter to Alexander Pope''
##*: I return you your Milton, which, upon '''collation''', I find to be revised, and augmented, in several places
##* '''1826''': {{w|Mary Shelley}}, ''The Last Man''
##*: The '''collation''' of philosophical opinions, the study of historical facts, the acquirement of languages, were at once my recreation, and the serious aim of my life.
## {{C|en|Publishing}} The act of [[collating]] pages or sheets of a book, or from printing etc. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## A [[collection]], a [[gathering]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
##* '''2010''', Will Dean, ''The Guardian'', 29 Apr 2010:
##*: It's fantastic, as is so much of Forgiveness Rock Record, a '''collation''' of so many talents that it's practically bursting at the seams.
# Discussion, light meal.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[conference]] or [[consultation]]. {{defdate|14th-17th c.}}
## {{lb|en|in the plural}} The ''Collationes Patrum in Scetica Eremo Commorantium'' by {{w|John Cassian}}, an important ecclesiastical work. (Now usually with capital initial.) {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##* '''1563''', John Foxe, ''Acts and Monuments'', vol. 2, p. 55:
##*: A certain abbot, named Moses, thus testifieth of himself in the '''Collations''' of Cassianus, that he so afflicted himself with much fasting and watching, that sometimes, for two or three days together, not only he felt no appetite to eat, but also had no remembrance of any meat at all {{...}}
## A [[reading]] held from the work mentioned above, as a regular service in Benedictine [[monastery|monasteries]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* '''1843''', TD Fosbroke, ''British Monachism'', p. 52:
##*: When the hymn was over the Sacrist was to strike the table for '''collation''', and the Deacon to enter with the Gospel, preceded by three converts, carrying the candlestick and censer.
## The light [[meal]] taken by [[monk]]s after the reading service mentioned above. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## Any light [[meal]] or [[snack]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Francesca Carrara|volume=1|page=138|text=Her first glance was one of triumph—her next was one of mingled admiration and gratitude for Louis; and, accepting his offered hand, they led the way to the banquet prepared in the Palais Orion,—a favourite garden-house, where they often had '''collations''' when the party was but small, which was the case to-day.}}
##* '''2008''', Tim Hayward, ''The Guardian'', 13 May 08:
##*: Yes, absolutely; supper, at least in English tradition, was a cold '''collation''', left out by cook before retiring.
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} The [[presentation]] of a [[clergyman]] to a [[benefice]] by a [[bishop]], who has it in his own gift.
# {{lb|en|civil law|inheritance}} The blending together of property so as to achieve equal division, mainly in the case of [[inheritance]].
#: {{syn|es|hotchpot}}
# {{lb|en|civil law|inheritance|Scotland}} An [[heir]]'s [[right]] to combine the whole [[heritable]] and [[movable estate]]s of the [[deceased]] into one mass, sharing it equally with others who are of the same degree of [[kindred]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of conferring or bestowing.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Of Empire}}
#*: Not by the '''collation''' of the king {{...}} but by the people.
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} [[presentation|Presentation]] to a [[benefice]].

